---
import Base from "../layouts/Base.astro";
import StageTrack from "../components/StageTrack.astro";
import ReportError from "../components/ReportError.astro";
import StatusMade from "../components/StatusMade.astro";
import { PAIR_SOURCES } from "../lib/pair-record";
import { statusSlug } from "../lib/guide";
import { SITE } from "../../site.config.mjs";
import { STAGE_PATH, STATUS_LABELS, STATUS_MEANINGS, overlayText } from "../lib/status";
import { NO_REPORT, setAbout } from "../lib/trial-page.mjs";
import { listing } from "../lib/trials-listing";

const codes = Object.keys(STATUS_LABELS) as (keyof typeof STATUS_LABELS)[];
---

<Base
  title="How it works"
  description="How a status is set from public records, what each status means, and what this site does not say."
>
  <h1 class="title">How it works</h1>
  <p class="lede">
    Each page follows one treatment for one condition and shows the public records found for it.
    This page explains how the status on a page is set and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  <StatusMade />

  {/* A long page on a phone: its sections, one tap away. */}
  <nav class="contents" aria-label="On this page">
    <ul>
      <li><a href="#how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-each-status-means">What each status means</a></li>
      <li><a href="#no-signal">No signal</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-a-trial-entry-shows">What a trial entry shows</a></li>
      {listing.trials.length > 0 && <li><a href="#a-trial-with-no-report">A trial with no report</a></li>}
      <li><a href="#what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</a></li>
      <li><a href="#where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</a></li>
      <li><a href="#an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</a></li>
      <li><a href="#ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</a></li>
      <li><a href="#what-is-counted">What is counted</a></li>
      {SITE.correctionsUrl && <li><a href="#report-an-error">Report an error</a></li>}
    </ul>
  </nav>

  <h2 class="bar" id="how-a-status-is-set">How a status is set</h2>
  <p>
    A status is derived by fixed rules from the approved evidence events listed on the page.
    Where no trial is found, the rules also use the kind of study the first reports described.
    No model and no editor decides a status. Every status lists the events that produced it.
  </p>
  <p>The rules are applied in this order, and the first one that fits sets the status.</p>
  <ol>
    <li>
      If the paper the first news reports were about has a retraction notice, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.retracted}”.
    </li>
    <li>If a regulator has recorded an approval, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.approved}”.</li>
    <li>
      Otherwise, trials that have not been terminated, withdrawn or marked as missing their primary
      endpoint are the ones that count. If any remain, the status follows the furthest one that has
      started: “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_2}” or “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_1}”.
      If none has started, or none has a phase, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.trial_registered}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and a person has marked a trial as missing its primary endpoint, with a
      source link, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.failed}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      If no trial remains and every registered trial was terminated or withdrawn, the status is
      “{STATUS_LABELS.terminated}”.
    </li>
    <li>
      With no trial found at all, the status depends on what the first reports described. If it was
      confirmed laboratory or animal work, the status is “{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”.
      A study in people, or an unconfirmed evidence stage, gives “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”.
    </li>
  </ol>
  <p>
    A treatment that is still at a stage open to further evidence (“{STATUS_LABELS.preclinical_only}”
    through “{STATUS_LABELS.phase_3}”, and “{STATUS_LABELS.no_trial_found}”) can also carry the
    no-signal note, described below.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-each-status-means">What each status means</h2>
  <dl class="meanings">
    {
      codes.map((code) => (
        <div>
          <dt>
            {STAGE_PATH.includes(code) && <StageTrack code={code} />}
            <a href={`/guide/${statusSlug(code)}`}>{STATUS_LABELS[code]}</a>
          </dt>
          <dd>{STATUS_MEANINGS[code]}</dd>
        </div>
      ))
    }
  </dl>
  <p class="entry-note">
    Each status links to its entry in <a href="/guide">the guide</a>, which says what it rests on
    and what it does not tell you.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="no-signal">No signal</h2>
  <p>
    A treatment that has stayed at an open stage for several years, with no new approved record, shows a
    note such as “{overlayText(5)}”. The note means that the most recent record is that old.
    The count starts from the date of that record, or from the date the treatment was first reported
    when no record has been found.
  </p>
  <p>
    It says nothing about work that has not been published or registered, and it is not a status.
    A new record removes it.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-a-trial-entry-shows">What a trial entry shows</h2>
  <p>
    A page lists each of its trials once. Each entry gives what the trial's registry record
    states: its state, its phase, how many people it enrolled or plans to enrol, when it started,
    when its main measure is due or was reached, the arms it compares, and whether results have
    been posted. The arm that tests the page's treatment is shown in bold. Where a trial stopped
    early and the record says why, the entry repeats the record's words. Under these are the
    dated changes in the record, such as the day it was registered or completed. These facts are
    read from the record by fixed rules. No editor and no AI model reads them.
  </p>
  <p>
    Posted results are the numbers the trial's sponsor gave the registry: each group's value, and
    any comparison the sponsor posted with it. The site shows the numbers and adds no word on
    what they mean for the treatment. They do not change a status.
  </p>
  <p>
    Where the trial's registry record names papers, the entry links them. A paper is not a trial
    record, and an entry never says what a paper concluded.
  </p>
  <p>
    An entry does not say where a trial takes place or who may take part in it.
  </p>

  {listing.trials.length > 0 && (
    <>
      <h2 class="bar" id="a-trial-with-no-report">A trial with no report</h2>
      <p>
        Most registered trials are never the subject of a news report, so no page here cites them.
        For {listing.trials.length.toLocaleString("en")} of them this site still has a page:{" "}
        {setAbout(listing.set, listing.records_read)}. Search finds them by registry number, or by
        words of their title, treatment or condition, after any page that follows a treatment.
      </p>
      <p>
        A study's state is the registry's own, as of the day the registry last changed the record,
        and that day is shown beside it. A record can be listed as not finished and not have been
        changed for years: this site does not check a study's state any other way.
      </p>
      <p>
        Such a page is read from the registry's own record when it is opened. It states what the
        record states and nothing more: the study's state, phase, conditions, treatments, size,
        sponsor and dates, whether results have been posted to the registry and when, and a link to
        the record. It reads “{NO_REPORT}” It carries no status, because a status belongs to a
        treatment for a condition, and no word on how the study turned out. It does not say where a study takes place
        or who may take part in it.
      </p>
    </>
  )}

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-this-site-never-says">What this site never says</h2>
  <p class="plain">
    This site gives no verdict on a treatment, a researcher, a company or a news outlet. It gives no
    medical advice and recommends no treatment. A registered clinical trial is not a treatment
    option.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="where-the-data-comes-from">Where the data comes from</h2>
  <p>
    Every record on a page links to the source that holds it. Records are shown as the
    registries and databases gave them. The wording of statuses is the site’s.
    The text of a news article is never copied or stored.
  </p>
  <ul>
    {
      PAIR_SOURCES.map((source) => (
        <li>
          <a href={source.url}>{source.name}</a>
        </li>
      ))
    }
  </ul>
  <p>
    A treatment reported under a brand name is looked up in
    <a href="https://www.nlm.nih.gov/research/umls/rxnorm/index.html">RxNorm</a> for the name of
    its ingredient, which is then used to search the trial registry. That source asks for the
    statement that follows: “This product uses publicly available data from the
    U.S. National Library of Medicine (NLM), National Institutes of Health, Department of Health
    and Human Services; NLM is not responsible for the product and does not endorse or recommend
    this or any other product.”
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="an-optional-search-of-a-new-report">An optional search of a new report</h2>
  <p>
    When you ask for a registry search, OpenAI reads the report's text to identify its treatment
    and condition. We search ClinicalTrials.gov and linked research through Europe PMC, then
    use OpenAI to compare the trial records. This is a live search of those research sources,
    not a general search of the web. Its answer is marked as automatic and unreviewed; it cannot
    create a page or change a status. We do not store submitted article text or screenshots.
    Structured answers for independently public reports may be reused for 24 hours; answers
    based only on browser content stay in that browser.
    Read <a href="/privacy">what is sent and how the providers handle it</a> before searching.
  </p>
  <p>
    Related papers come from OpenAlex searches for the treatment and condition. ROR identifies
    organisations explicitly named in the report. These sections open when you need them.
    A paper result does not establish that a treatment works, and an organisation record does
    not verify involvement or endorsement. Neither provider receives the article text, page
    address or screenshot. Public paper and organisation metadata may be cached for seven days.
  </p>
  <p>
    Opening the browser extension checks the current page automatically. A tracked article shows
    the page that follows it. After you enable automatic search, an untracked page starts a live registry
    search when the service is available. If its text is insufficient, a screenshot of visible
    content can be sent. Select words first to search only those words, without links or a screenshot.
    Each candidate's match confidence is the model's estimate that the record concerns the reported
    treatment and condition. It is not a verified probability or a rating of treatment effectiveness
    or trial quality. Pages are not searched in the background while the extension is closed.
  </p>

  <h2 class="bar" id="ways-to-keep-up">Ways to keep up</h2>
  <p>
    A status can change months or years after a headline. Neither of these needs an account, and
    neither tells us who you are.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>A feed.</strong> For a feed reader: <a href="/changes.xml">every status change</a>.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>The history on a page.</strong> Each page lists, under "How the status changed",
      every change of its status with the day it was seen.
    </li>
  </ul>

  <h2 class="bar" id="what-is-counted">What is counted</h2>
  <p class="plain">Cloudflare, which hosts this site, counts requests and errors to operate the service. We do not add analytics, set tracking cookies, or keep reader profiles. Request logging for our Worker is disabled.</p>

  {
    SITE.correctionsUrl && (
      <>
        <h2 class="bar" id="report-an-error">Report an error</h2>
        <p>An error on any page of this site is reported the same way. Say which page it is on.</p>
        <ReportError page="/how-it-works" />
      </>
    )
  }
</Base>
